**Theorem 5.2 (current-A fibre after the varying latch is gone).** All
$2^{r_{\rm len}}$ histories $h_w$ have identical $P_A^+$. Their original
$Q$ responses to the single prescribed Advance, the final original
payload WR(13,5), are pairwise distinct. Hence no memory factoring
through $P_A^+$ satisfies even memory equality implies original-task
equality on a domain containing this family.

Proof. CAP Definition 4.6 acquires the header and unary length, allocates
the $d$-bit payload and then executes
RD(length), IN(5), WR(13,5), MR(payload), MR(length).
There is no branch on a payload value. Every binary address of length
$d$ is a lawful TagRead payload, including descents later returning
absent. Therefore every family member has the same paid setup, native
row sequence, head motions, finite tests, origins and creation positions
through this cut. During its first $r_{\rm len}$ complete payload
iterations only the corresponding value symbols on producer 13 vary.
Their WRs change no seal, head, descriptor, token or A material.

ARC $F^A$ has no history-bank assignment and makes no request dependency
delivery before the complete frame and ensuing original source service.
The original 24/25/27 remain parked; 26 is unchanged; 28–31 have received
nothing from this request. Added 64–73, prior acquired packages and
their rights are identical, as is the already retained common gate 01.
The final payload length RD reads the same positive counter bit and the
final IN supplies 0 in every case. This overwrites the earlier varying
input latch. All other latches have the same last writes, including the
length-test latch, so every finite bit agrees. Receipt symbols depend
on the common row/control schedule, not the payload value; the whole
meter word agrees. All heads and token/owner fields also agree.
Consequently the strengthened owner views are equal.

At these cuts WR(13,5) is enabled with the actual producer token. Its
actual effect writes the same final 0 and preserves the preceding $w$.
The row is the separately numbered inlining of the original CAP payload
WR: ARC Definition 4.1 changes only the graph's final return CTL, and
Definition 6.2 preserves this row's operand, guard and successor effect.
Projecting its successor to the original CAP coordinates still includes
port 13, with the same original cell identities and differing $w$.
AIF Definition 2.6 explicitly retains this complete original joint
successor in the Advance response. Thus distinct $w$ give unequal
original $Q$ responses, without reading a new history coordinate or
assuming added-phase task rigidity. If a factor of $P_A^+$ were
sufficient, its equal values would force these unequal responses to
agree, a contradiction. The family is canonical, preterminal and
ordinary; no final-halt or Ready-horizon argument is used. ∎

